WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.522
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.522 --> 00:00:02.480
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello.

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:06.240
I'm Dustin Kirkland here at
KubeCon in Copenhagen, Denmark.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.970
And I'm joined by
Tim Hockin, who's

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:10.610
going to give us his thoughts
on a handful of the questions

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.890
that you've been
asking us this week.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:16.224
Tim, you've been around
Kubernetes a long time.

00:00:16.224 --> 00:00:18.140
I'd really like to hear
from you in your words

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:21.560
how Kubernetes is changing
the course of application

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:25.310
development and ultimately
IT operations at scale.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:26.510
TIM HOCKIN: Sure.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:29.630
I think Kubernetes
containers and the sort

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:32.240
of orchestration model
is really changing

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.176
the way people are able to
think about their applications,

00:00:35.176 --> 00:00:36.800
the way they think
about building them,

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.595
and the way they think
about running them.

00:00:38.595 --> 00:00:40.220
In terms of building,
Kubernetes really

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.560
captures the
microservices model where

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.030
you can build lots
of small pieces

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.930
and link them together
into a larger application.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.720
From the operations point
of view, from the IT side,

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.510
it lets you think about your
applications and your code

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.774
in a way that isn't
so one to one.

00:00:55.774 --> 00:00:58.190
You don't think about which
machine is running which piece

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.290
of software, and you
don't think about how

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:03.278
you handle events and sorts
of things that go wrong.

00:01:03.278 --> 00:01:05.069
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
why is that important?

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:06.440
TIM HOCKIN: It's important
because things go wrong

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:06.991
all the time.

00:01:06.991 --> 00:01:07.490
Right?

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.698
I mean everybody who's run
an application understands

00:01:09.698 --> 00:01:10.340
the reality.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.380
Things crash, and they
always crash at 2 o'clock

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:16.840
in the morning on a Sunday
when you are on vacation

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.580
after a night of drinking.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:19.380
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
But with Kubernetes,

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.255
that doesn't happen?

00:01:20.255 --> 00:01:20.880
TIM HOCKIN: No.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.480
Things still crash,
but Kubernetes

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:25.200
is designed to accept the
reality that things will crash.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.825
And we will just move
it somewhere else

00:01:26.825 --> 00:01:29.870
because the identity of the
machine that you're running on

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:31.280
is not that important.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.990
Your application is
just a unit that we

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:35.039
can move across the
soup of your cluster,

00:01:35.039 --> 00:01:36.830
and we can just bring
it up somewhere else.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:38.360
We can let the system self-heal.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.370
And if it takes a little
while for that crashed machine

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.190
to come back online,
maybe it takes a week,

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:44.106
maybe you have to buy a
new hard drive, that's

00:01:44.106 --> 00:01:46.680
fine because you shifted
the load somewhere else.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.220
And once people start to
internalize that idea,

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:50.990
it changes fundamentally
the way you

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.770
think about running your
applications in the cluster.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:57.770
This is how we've been doing it
at Google more or less forever.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:01.640
The idea of a machine, a
VM or a physical machine,

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.040
a server that runs my
application and just

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.850
my application is completely
foreign to the Google mindset

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:09.560
of how you build an application.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:13.050
You throw it into the Borg
and it runs somewhere.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.470
And so Kubernetes tries
to bring the best of that

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:16.580
to the open source world.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.170
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So Tim, you've
been around this technology

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:19.050
for a long time.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.820
You've seen the evolution
of Borg at Google.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:27.200
And in its early inception and
its migration into Kubernetes,

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.170
how did Google Developers
make that transition

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:31.280
to writing for Borg?

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.730
And how does that translate to
third party developers writing

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:38.230
for a Kubernetes that maybe
has nothing to do with--

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.730
they're quite detached from the
Kubernetes that's ultimately

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.660
going to run their application?

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.870
TIM HOCKIN: So with
Google and Googlers,

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:48.500
it was back in 2003-ish
when Borg started

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.052
to come into fruition,
and it really

00:02:50.052 --> 00:02:52.854
was a very different
system than it is now,

00:02:52.854 --> 00:02:54.020
but the ideas were the same.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.690
Take a machine, share it
across multiple users,

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.490
put some isolation
barriers in place

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.530
so that you can provide
some level of guarantee

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.440
that you won't hurt each
other, that you can co-exist.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.750
And it took a long
time for people

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:09.320
to give up the private
machine mentality, especially

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.110
for these big applications,
like search, where Google really

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:13.670
makes its money.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.575
These were very difficult. They
have very strong requirements

00:03:16.575 --> 00:03:18.200
in terms of performance
and guarantees.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:19.908
And so it took a long
time for those apps

00:03:19.908 --> 00:03:22.490
to come into the Borg ecosystem.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:25.640
But once they did, their
lives are better for it.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.620
They get the
consistent operations,

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.300
the existing tooling.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.360
We have a very small cluster--

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.620
sorry-- very small team
of cluster administrators

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.000
who run the entire Google fleet.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.280
And it's amazing because
the power of Borg

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.660
lets them scale that way.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.320
So for people who are
outside Google, people

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:45.950
who are in the real world,
they look at Kubernetes,

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:47.900
and they see it
as this new thing.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.340
They don't really know
what to make of it.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.730
It's natural that people have
some amount of reluctance

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:53.930
to get into it.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:56.750
But the more we show people,
the better off they will be.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:58.910
I had a great
personal moment when

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:02.137
I was presenting at USENIX
LISA a couple of years ago,

00:04:02.137 --> 00:04:03.220
room full of systems and--

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:04.960
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Fantastic
conference, yeah.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:06.740
TIM HOCKIN: And I showed them
some of what Kubernetes can do.

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:07.910
Just here's how you
do a rolling update.

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:08.900
Here's how you do a deployment.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.910
Here's how you flip a
service from A to B,

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.850
and there were audible
gasps in the room.

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:15.620
How did you do that?

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:18.069
And that was great for me
because this is the mindset.

00:04:18.069 --> 00:04:19.610
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
I've seen that, too.

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:21.890
The developers who
come around and come

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:24.590
to understand how much
Kubernetes does for them so

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:26.720
that they can focus on
simply their application

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:30.020
packages as a container
and see that scaled out.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.940
I think it helps
take, you mentioned,

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:34.820
it helps taking a
small IT team and turn

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:38.150
it functional at larger
scale even smaller,

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:41.120
but also helps one developer
or team of developers

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:43.275
create quite a bit
more technology.

00:04:43.275 --> 00:04:44.150
TIM HOCKIN: For sure.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.860
You don't need to
have a huge operations

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:47.310
team to run a big fleet.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:50.840
And I think you can get away
with a very small ops team

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.030
if you've got a small fleet.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:55.100
If you've got 100 nodes
in your fleet, which

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:56.960
is a pretty decent
sized compute fleet,

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.100
you can run a pretty big
website on 100 machines,

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.710
and you can really run that
with one or two people.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:05.050
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So you've seen
Google effectively democratize

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:07.380
that technology and make
that generally available

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.610
to practically anyone.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:09.568
TIM HOCKIN: Absolutely.

00:05:09.568 --> 00:05:12.570
I mean a big part of Kubernetes
was bringing these ideas

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.920
forward, but not just
the ideas on their own,

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:16.310
like a working implementation.

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:18.464
It was really important
for us to say, not only do

00:05:18.464 --> 00:05:20.130
we believe you should
operate like this,

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:22.950
but we're going to
help you do this.

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.720
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Now
you've got a position

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.510
in the community
that enables you

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:31.020
to see a lot of the different,
hard, technical problems

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.010
around Kubernetes.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:35.760
Can you give us a sense
of where some of the areas

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:37.440
are that need the most work?

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.110
And I'm talking less
code writing and more

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:43.020
like design decisions, some
of the big, hard problems.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.010
TIM HOCKIN: Sure.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.480
Kubernetes is a big project.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.514
It's got a lot of surface area.

00:05:47.514 --> 00:05:48.930
One of the big
problems we have is

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.740
how to limit the growth
of that surface area.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:54.840
We can't grow forever.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.940
And so a ton of
work has been going

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.760
into things like extension
mechanisms and plug-ins

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.210
and hooks and making
those things really truly

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.370
usable for production
grade deployments, not just

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.080
prototypes.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:08.940
And so a ton of work
has been going in.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.390
A ton more work has to be
done and is being done now.

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:15.729
The community's amazing
and very vibrant here.

00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:17.770
So this is one of the
thing's really interesting.

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:21.750
Another is in the growth
of the surface area,

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:24.180
you see a lot of new
challenges that were not

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:26.024
in scope when we started.

00:06:26.024 --> 00:06:28.440
I work in the infrastructure
side of things, so networking

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:29.050
and storage.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.100
So one example here
is Kubernetes growing

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.942
to fill more networking spaces.

00:06:33.942 --> 00:06:35.400
Things that have
traditionally been

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:38.430
filled by classical,
virtualized networking,

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.610
NFVs, and virtual firewalls
and deep packet inspection

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:44.140
and all those sorts of things
that a lot of enterprises,

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:47.460
a lot of big companies
have been sold

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.530
that they believe in the
technology, the value

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:50.940
that it brings to their company.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.700
How do you map
that to Kubernetes?

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:54.570
And you know--

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:55.110
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Is it a place where

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:58.050
Kubernetes has to plug into
the way the enterprise already

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:58.552
operates?

00:06:58.552 --> 00:07:00.010
It can't just wipe
the slate clean.

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:00.660
TIM HOCKIN: Some of it.

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:01.420
Some of that.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:04.230
And some of it is really looking
at the problem with fresh eyes

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:05.620
and coming back
to it and saying,

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:08.739
what would be the Kubernetes
way of describing this problem?

00:07:08.739 --> 00:07:10.530
So there's going to be
some blending there.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:12.460
These are problems
that Google never had.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:14.700
This is a place where Borg
just doesn't have anything

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.740
because it's not a problem
that Google deals with.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:18.656
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: What
are some of the projects

00:07:18.656 --> 00:07:20.310
that you find most
interesting or some

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:22.268
of the interesting
innovations and developments

00:07:22.268 --> 00:07:23.940
that are happening?

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.340
TIM HOCKIN: I'm a big
fan of the Istio project.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:28.530
And not just because it
partly comes out of Google,

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:31.200
but I think the technology
is really interesting, really

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:32.700
exciting.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.040
The demonstrations
that they can show you,

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.180
like look, we can
do this for you,

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:40.290
is on the level of the first
demonstrations of Kubernetes

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:42.720
of saying, look, we
can do this for you.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.390
So it sort of gives
me that childlike glee

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:48.670
when I see somebody doing
something really cool.

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:50.520
There are so many
other projects.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:53.100
I mentioned earlier the
networking and devices.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:55.840
There's all the work around
ML and GPUs that is happening.

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:57.360
I think it's really
exciting stuff.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:59.340
It's a really hot
space that's going

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.240
to really change the world
and probably obsolete me.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.640
And its exciting to see these
people working on things,

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.399
but we got to figure out the
sort of principled models

00:08:08.399 --> 00:08:09.440
with which we're working.

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:11.190
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: It
seems to be a good fit

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:12.360
so far with Kubernetes.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.318
TIM HOCKIN: I can't
imagine it not being a fit,

00:08:14.318 --> 00:08:17.015
but there's previous decisions
that we have to work through

00:08:17.015 --> 00:08:19.140
in terms of compatibility
and in terms of how do we

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:21.739
fit these things in the system.

00:08:21.739 --> 00:08:23.280
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
And then, who do you

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:26.935
find some of the most inspiring
people around the community?

00:08:26.935 --> 00:08:28.560
TIM HOCKIN: The most
inspiring people--

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:30.120
I'm an engineer
through and through.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:33.990
So I really look up to
folks like Clayton Coleman

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:37.770
from Red Hat, who is really
so fundamental to the project.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.240
The project would
not be what it is

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:42.480
without him and his
team of awesome people

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:43.947
that they brought
to the project.

00:08:43.947 --> 00:08:45.780
But I also look around
at some of the people

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:49.640
I work with at Google and
that I measure myself against.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.980
There's guys like Brian
Grant, who is just a genius,

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:54.630
and Eric Tune, who
is just the best

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.880
engineer I've ever worked with.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.571
These people are amazing to me.

00:08:58.571 --> 00:09:00.570
And then, you look around
the rest of the space,

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:03.670
and there's all of these
brilliant, exciting people who

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:06.690
are out talking about Kubernetes
and evangelizing and showing

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:07.490
neat ideas.

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:09.240
And you don't need to
be core contributors

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.340
to be really exciting.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:14.040
It's hard to pick any one.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:16.170
I tend to pick, I
guess, the people

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.620
I interact with the most,
but there's so many--

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:20.370
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Quite
a vibrant community

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:20.940
from that perspective.

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:21.300
TIM HOCKIN: So many people.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:23.700
I'm going to spend all week
at KubeCon talking to people.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:25.319
I doubt if I'll go
to a single talk.

00:09:25.319 --> 00:09:27.360
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And then,
do you have any advice

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:30.480
for maybe a university
student or a young engineer

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:32.260
just getting started
with Kubernetes?

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:35.079
How do they take
that first step?

00:09:35.079 --> 00:09:37.620
TIM HOCKIN: How do you take a
first step into any open source

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:38.120
project?

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:38.790
You just start.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:40.470
You just decide
you're going to do it.

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:43.620
You decide a problem that
you want to try to work on,

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:45.264
and you start reading code.

00:09:45.264 --> 00:09:47.180
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Get
cloned is the first step.

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:47.610
TIM HOCKIN: Get cloned.

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:48.110
Exactly.

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:50.187
And if you can start
from main, and if you

00:09:50.187 --> 00:09:52.020
can start reading code
and comprehending it,

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.686
and you go an hour and you don't
find something you need to fix,

00:09:54.686 --> 00:09:56.270
you're really not
looking hard enough.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:09:57.395
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: I like it.

00:09:57.395 --> 00:09:58.920
And then finally,
a question we're

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:01.020
asking all of our
interviewees here.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:04.210
How did-- I have to stifle
a little bit of a laugh--

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:08.147
how did you get started
with Kubernetes?

00:10:08.147 --> 00:10:10.480
TIM HOCKIN: So I was working
on the Borg team at Google.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.960
Specifically, I was working on
the node side of the Borg team

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:17.240
and Omega working
on the node agent

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:19.780
so the equivalent of
kubelet in the omega side

00:10:19.780 --> 00:10:21.580
was called omlet.

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:25.450
And we were working
on this, and we

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:28.270
were asked to work more
closely with a Cloud team

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:32.050
to see if we could find ways to
synergize to not duplicate work

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:33.620
so much.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:35.662
And at the same time,
this Docker thing landed.

00:10:35.662 --> 00:10:37.120
People started
talking with Docker.

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:39.880
And we looked at it, and said,
wow, this is kind of neat.

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:44.350
It's sort of what we've got
going on inside omlet, wordlet.

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:47.320
I could sort of see how this
could turn into an open source

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:50.500
Borg, which is something that
we had pitched to our leadership

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:53.980
before, but we didn't really
have the momentum behind it.

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:56.530
And then, a few folks
at Google took this

00:10:56.530 --> 00:10:57.880
and ran with a prototype.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:00.340
And they showed us this
very simple mock-up

00:11:00.340 --> 00:11:02.450
that they threw together,
and it was amazing.

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:03.970
It was immediate,
like I see this.

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:04.795
I get it.

00:11:04.795 --> 00:11:06.550
This is something.

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:08.830
And so the opportunity to
work on this was there.

00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:12.040
And I said I would like to
help take this from prototype

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:12.590
into reality.

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:13.506
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Cool.

00:11:13.506 --> 00:11:15.320
And now I want to
eat an omelette.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:17.170
I don't know why.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:17.740
All right.

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:18.270
Thanks Tim.

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:20.270
I appreciate it,
appreciate your time here.

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:21.720
TIM HOCKIN: Thanks.

